iven-names></name><name><surname>Hanrath</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Changes of beta-adrenergic signaling in compensated human cardiac hypertrophy depend on the underlying disease</article-title><source>Am J Physiol Heart Circ Physiol</source><year>2000</year><volume>278</volume><fpage>H2076</fpage><lpage>83</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10843907</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Freeman</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Lerman</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><name><surname>Kranias</surname><given-names>EG</given-names></name><name><surname>Bohlmeyer</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Bristow</surname><given-names>MR</given-names></name><name><surname>Lefkowitz</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Iaccarino</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Koch</surname><given-names>WJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Leinwand</surname><given-names>LA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Alterations in cardiac adrenergic signaling and calcium cycling differentially affect the progression of cardiomyopathy</article-title><source>J Clin Invest</source><year>2001</year><volume>107</volume><fpage>967</fpage><lpage>74</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11306600</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B14"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Liggett</surname><given-names>SB</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Beta-adrenergic receptors in the failing heart: the good, the bad, and the unknown</article-title><source>J Clin Invest</source><year>2001</year><volume>107</volume><fpage>947</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11306597</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B15"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lefkowitz</surname><given-names>RJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Rockman</surname><given-names>HA</given-names></name><name><surname>Koch</surname><given-names>WJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Catecholamines, cardiac beta-adrenergic receptors, and heart failure</article-title><source>Circulation</source><year>2000</year><volume>101</volume><fpage>1634</fpage><lpage>7</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10758041</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B16"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Paulson</surname><given-names>DJ</given-names></name><name><surname>Shetlar</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Light</surname><given-names>KE</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Catecholamine levels in the heart, serum and adrenals of experimental diabetic rats</article-title><source>Fed Proc</source><year>1980</year><volume>39</volume><fpage>637</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="B17"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Christensen</surname><given-names>NJ</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Plasma norepinephrine and epinephrine in untreated diabetics during fasting and after insulin administration</article-title><source>Diabetes</source><year>1974</year><volume>23</volume><fpage>1</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4203518</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="B18"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Brodde</surname><given-names>OE</given-names></name><name><surname>O'Hara</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><name><surname>Zerkowski</surname><given-names>HR</given-names></name><name><surname>Rohm</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Human cardiac beta-adrenoceptors: both beta 1- and beta 2-adrenoceptors are functionally coupled to the adenylate cyclase in right atrium</article-title><source>J Ca